## Rules

1. **The manuscript is read-only.** Nothing under `paper/` is edited, moved, renamed, or deleted,
   including code or data the authors supplied.
2. **Never compile LaTeX.** The referee compiles [where, e.g. on Overleaf]; when they say the
   report is compiled, read the PDF they put in `report/round-N/`, and never rebuild or overwrite
   it. Keep the report self-contained: standard packages only, no files outside its folder.
3. **No Python** unless the referee approves it for a specific document operation, each time.
   **Never run the authors' code or experiments.**
4. **Confidentiality.** The submitted material, including any code or data, and the notes derived
   from it are confidential review material. Never send their text, equations, figures, data, or
   distinctive unpublished details to web search or any outside service. General literature
   searches never contain submission content. [Journal-specific terms, e.g. on AI tools, as
   recorded in the brief.]
5. **No claims about the literature from memory.** Before a deliverable says anything about a
   paper, read its PDF from [the reference locations, in search order] and record it in
   `notes/litrev/`. Anything not yet checked is marked UNVERIFIED and stays out of the report.
   Never fabricate a reference to recommend.
6. **Every report comment is anchored and verified.** It names its location and is checked against
   the original file before it enters the report. Findings go to a note first; the report takes
   only those the referee has accepted.
7. **Anonymity.** Nothing in a file the authors see identifies the referee, PDF metadata included.
8. **Notes** — flat, topical, undated, one per open topic, updated in place. Settled notes move to
   `notes/done/`, with the round in the filename. See `<toolkit>/guides/project-hub.md`.
9. **Keep this hub current** — finishing a task means updating **Status** and **Active notes**.
   This file points; it never accumulates findings, session history, or summaries.

